% Origin: Kowalski, *Computational Logic and Human Thinking*,
%         Chapter 5 "Negation as Failure", section 5.2
%         ("The last-train conditional").
% Last verification: no open issues (no errors/warnings; all tests pass).
%
% Book rule: "the last train from victoria to pulborough leaves at 22:52 on a day
% if the day is a weekday and the day is in the period between 17 may 2010 and
% 12 december 2010." We model the time 22:52 as the string "22:52" and use the
% system date-range templates for the period. "is a weekday" facts and the
% candidate dates live in the scenarios (a weekday in range -> a train; an
% out-of-range or weekend date -> no train).

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
*a date* is a weekday.
the last train from victoria to pulborough leaves at *a time* on *a date*.

the knowledge base last_train includes:

the last train from victoria to pulborough leaves at "22:52" on a date
    if the date is a weekday
    and the date is after or equal to 2010-05-17
    and the date is before or equal to 2010-12-12.

scenario weekday_in_range is:
    2010-06-15 is a weekday.
    leaves expects answers ["the last train from victoria to pulborough leaves at 22:52 on 2010-6-15T0:0:0.0"].

scenario out_of_range is:
    2011-01-03 is a weekday.
    leaves expects answers [].

% 2010-06-13 was a Sunday: it is in range but NOT a weekday, so no train runs.
scenario weekend_in_range is:
    leaves expects answers [].

query leaves is:
    the last train from victoria to pulborough leaves at which time on which date.
